    id: "378762c45f75e88a891d",
    cat: "geopolitica",
    categories: ["geopolitica"],
    title: "France accuses Iran of supermarket fridge hacking",
    summary: "The cyber-attack last month saw fridges and freezers in hundreds of supermarkets across France heated up.",
    preview: "France has accused Iran's Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps (IRGC) of being behind cyber-attacks that raised the temperatures of supermarket fridges and freezers across the country last month. French Foreign Minister Jean-Noël Barrot said the attack was \"unprecedented in both its scale and its level of aggression\", adding those who were responsible would be hit with sanctions \"at the European level\". Iran is yet to respond to Barrot's allegations.",
    body: "France has accused Iran's Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps (IRGC) of being behind cyber-attacks that raised the temperatures of supermarket fridges and freezers across the country last month.\n\nFrench Foreign Minister Jean-Noël Barrot said the attack was \"unprecedented in both its scale and its level of aggression\", adding those who were responsible would be hit with sanctions \"at the European level\".\n\nIran is yet to respond to Barrot's allegations.\n\nThe cyber-attack last month saw fridges and freezers in hundreds of supermarkets across France heated up, which spoilt fresh and frozen food estimated to be worth millions of euros.\n\nThen last week, dozens of supermarkets across several other European countries were also impacted by a cyber-attack that targeted fridges and freezers. It is not known if the instances are linked.\n\nBarrot said the attack on France's supermarkets \"marks the first large-scale cyber-attack aimed at sabotage on our national territory, and it constitutes a major attack on the nation's interests and the security of the French people\".\n\nHe said the government had summoned the Iranian ambassador to Paris to \"lodge a very strong protest against this extremely grave act\".\n\nFrance will take \"any other necessary and proportionate measures\" to respond to the incident and \"deter anyone who might seek to repeat it\", Barrot added.\n\nSeparately on Monday, the Paris prosecutor said an investigation had been opened into the head of an offensive cyber unit associated with Iran's Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps (IRGC) as well as his two deputies.\n\nLaure Beccuau named the unit as Shahid Kaveh, which she said had an affiliation with the electronic and cyber centre of the IRGC.\n\nShahid Kaveh is the name given to a cluster of hackers thought to be directly employed by the Iranian regime.\n\nOn 11 October, hackers gained control of the software that regulates the temperatures of the refrigeration units used in many of France's supermarkets.\n\nMore than 100 of Coopérative U's supermarkets were hit, as well as 180 stores from Intermarché and its Netto brand. In some stores, temperatures of the fridges reportedly rose to 50C (120F) overnight.\n\nOther major food retailers including Carrefour, Monoprix and E.Leclerc were also hit by the attack, which targeted software provided by Danish engineering firm Danfoss.\n\nThe software allows retailers to manage their cold units remotely.\n\nEarlier this month a second wave of hacks took place, hitting more than 100 stores across Europe.\n\nSupermarkets in Belgium and Luxembourg were affected, while a smaller number of shops were hit in countries including Sweden, Ireland, the Netherlands, Estonia and Finland.\n\nSeparately in August a small number of US military bases had fridges mysteriously malfunction, spoiling food. This incident is also being investigated as deliberate cyber-attacks, Reuters news agency has reported, citing sources familiar with the matter.\n\nSince the waves of attacks on freezers, experts have been speculating which hacking groups could be responsible, with Iran being on a list of potential suspects.\n\nIn spite of a pair of Russian-aligned hacktivist groups claiming they were responsible on Wednesday, few believed them.\n\nThe French government has not said how it reached the conclusion that Iran was responsible, and attribution of cyber attacks is extremely hard as hackers are able to hide their locations.\n\nRelated topics\n\n- Published7 days ago",
    content_status: "full",
    source: "BBC World",
    url: "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cmg474vk6dyqo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
