    id: "204e4325ea30c0e456d2",
    cat: "geopolitica",
    categories: ["geopolitica"],
    title: "Strait of Hormuz still a source of leverage for Iran",
    summary: "Attacks in the Strait of Hormuz put crews at risk and drive up energy shipping costs. Iran's ability to sustain them and the limits of US protection will shape how much trade can continue and at what price.",
    preview: "November 9, 2026 Attacks in the Strait of Hormuz put crews at risk and drive up energy shipping costs. Iran's ability to sustain them and the limits of US protection will shape how much trade can continue and at what price. Since the Iran war started, attacks on commercial ships in the Strait of Hormuz have put crews at risk of death and injury, forcing shipping companies to choose whether to attempt the passage or delay cargos and face higher costs.",
    body: "November 9, 2026\n\nAttacks in the Strait of Hormuz put crews at risk and drive up energy shipping costs. Iran's ability to sustain them and the limits of US protection will shape how much trade can continue and at what price.\n\nSince the Iran war started, attacks on commercial ships in the Strait of Hormuz have put crews at risk of death and injury, forcing shipping companies to choose whether to attempt the passage or delay cargos and face higher costs.\n\nMerchant vessels continue to use the strait despite the danger, but how long that can continue depends partly on Iran's ability to sustain its attacks and the United States' capacity to protect shipping and bear the cost.\n\nAli Mohammadi, an expert in Iranian affairs based in France, believes Iran's geography and the equipment it uses allow it to keep attacking ships.\n\nBut he sees the economic burden of the war as Tehran's main constraint.\n\n\"Iran's limitation in the Strait of Hormuz is economic rather than military, because continuing the war has a significant impact on its economy,\" he said.\n\nIt's unclear how long Iran can sustain attacks given the lack of reliable information on the nation's weapons stocks and its ability to replenish supplies. And sporadic attacks in the crucial waterway are also different from closing the strait completely.\n\n\"Even if a complete closure of the Strait of Hormuz were possible, it would not be sustainable because it could carry high political costs for Iran,\" Mohammadi told DW. \"Iran cannot indefinitely disrupt its allies' access to the strait.\"\n\nBy threatening shipping, Iran can raise the cost of confrontation and seek leverage in negotiations, but that may not guarantee success or yield concessions, the expert said.\n\nMore crossings, but traffic remains depressed\n\nData from the UK Maritime Trade Operations Center's (UKMTO) weekly report, released on November 6, show 325 reported transits through Hormuz between October 29 and November 4. That was up from 277 the previous week, an increase of about 17%.\n\nIt's still roughly 65% below pre-war levels, the UKMTO assessed.\n\nBut vessel numbers alone cannot explain what is happening to energy supplies as cargo volumes and the routes used also matter, said Dalga Khatinoglu, an expert on Iran's energy and macroeconomics.\n\n\"If we look only at crude oil, Middle Eastern exports across all routes, from the Gulf of Oman and the Strait of Hormuz to the Red Sea, have largely recovered to pre-war levels,\" he told DW. \"In some weeks in September and October, they even exceeded those levels.\"\n\nHis assessment covers the region's overall crude exports across all routes, not just shipments passing through the Strait of Hormuz.\n\nThe picture for refined petroleum products is different, Khatinoglu said. \"Before the war, the Middle East exported about 6.2 million barrels of petroleum products a day. That figure has now roughly halved.\"\n\nLiquefied natural gas (LNG) exports have also suffered. \"As of September, exports from Qatar and the UAE were about 10% below pre-war levels. Although there has been some improvement, they remain severely disrupted,\" the expert underscored.\n\nThe cost of getting through spikes\n\nKhatinoglu said recovering energy exports have not eliminated the costs associated with ongoing insecurity.\n\n\"Although oil exports from Middle Eastern countries have approached pre-war levels, shipping and insurance costs have remained very high,\" he stressed.\n\nCiting recent figures from the Organization of the Petroleum Exporting Countries (OPEC), he said that average freight rates for a large tanker carrying crude from the Middle East to East Asia has risen about 12-fold, from $3.18 (€2.84) per barrel at the beginning of the year to $37.13 a barrel at the end of October.\n\nThis means elevated costs for Asian importers even when crude is available.\n\nReducing costs and restoring commercial confidence in the safety of the strait requires more than a US military presence, Mohammadi said.\n\n\"There is only one solution to the Strait of Hormuz issue and that's an end to the war, accompanied by a lasting agreement that brings lasting security,\" he said.\n\nKhatinoglu shared a similar view. \"You cannot expect oil prices to return to pre-war levels simply by relying on US protection for ships in the Strait of Hormuz,\" he said.",
    content_status: "full",
    source: "Deutsche Welle",
    url: "https://www.dw.com/en/strait-of-hormuz-still-a-source-of-leverage-for-iran/a-79901647?maca=en-rss-en-world-4025-rdf",
